  "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}y=-1 & |\\times 3 \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{7}y=\\frac{1}{7} & |\\times 7\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}4x+y=-3 \\\\ 7x+3y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the first equation can easily be solved for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-3-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y=-3-4x$ into the second equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+3y & =1 \\\\ 7x+3(-3-4x) & =1 \\\\ 7x-9-12x & =1 \\\\ -5x & =10 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $y,$ we substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $y=-3-4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3-4x \\\\  & =-3-4(-2) \\\\  & =-3+8 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2$ and $y=5.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
